## Mechanism and scoped correction

Both original `afterAll` hooks resolve a sibling diskSteward Python script
relative to the checkout. That sibling is absent beside the recovery worktree.
The script-existence assertion fails after the actual database assertions have
already passed. This is a test lifecycle dependency, not a new compound-save
or authentication failure.

Only scratch creation and lifecycle code in these two tests changes:

- `compound-conflict-connections.attack.test.ts`
- `compound-current-external-state.attack.test.ts`

Creation now uses the existing repository `scratchDir` factory. Before any
explicit removal, the tests require their own registration and matching
checkout/machine ownership manifest. Every actual driver handle still closes
in `afterEach`, with an additional empty-handle assertion before teardown.
Only the owned flat directory is measured. The test's teardown removes it
through `removeScratchDir` if shared teardown has not already done so.
The original explicit absence assertion remains and fails visibly if removal
fails. There is no machine-neighbor command, hardcoded machine path or Python
dependency in either corrected file.

The full `fixture` function and everything following it are byte-identical to
the root originals in both files. Thus all 16 expanded test bodies, original
semantic assertions, two real SQLite connections, actual registered core
events, snapshots and rollback checks remain unchanged. No application module,
driver, shared scratch helper, global guard, approval policy or hook is edited.

The existing scratch helper was read in full before use. Its immediate remove
operation does not itself enforce registration and swallows an unsuccessful
remove; the tests therefore check registration/manifest themselves and retain
the required final absence assertion. Its keep/debug behavior cannot silently
make these cleanup tests green. The four-line ownership/absence contract is
local to directories this test creates, not a new general deletion authority.

